WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.771
A word that means too many things.

2
00:00:02.051 --> 00:00:06.253
“Agent” is the most overloaded word in AI engineering right now.

3
00:00:06.533 --> 00:00:09.307
Vendors call a chatbot with one tool an agent.

4
00:00:09.587 --> 00:00:13.256
Researchers use it for a system that plans and acts over hours.

5
00:00:13.536 --> 00:00:17.760
Your colleagues use it for anything that seems to do more than answer a question.

6
00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:28.024
That ambiguity has a cost: teams commit to “building an agent” before anyone has said what the system should decide on its own, and they discover the answer in production.

7
00:00:28.304 --> 00:00:41.765
This studio uses a precise definition, taken from Anthropic’s engineering post “Building effective agents” (Erik Schluntz and Barry Zhang, December 2024), which has become a common reference point.

8
00:00:42.045 --> 00:00:47.720
The post groups everything under the umbrella of agentic systems and then draws one line through it.

9
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.589
Workflows are systems where models and tools are orchestrated through predefined code paths.

10
00:00:53.869 --> 00:00:56.003
Your code decides what happens next.

11
00:00:56.283 --> 00:01:02.661
A model may write, classify, or check at each step, but the sequence and the set of branches are fixed by you.

12
00:01:02.941 --> 00:01:11.155
Agents are systems where the model dynamically directs its own process and tool use, keeping control over how it accomplishes the task.

13
00:01:11.435 --> 00:01:19.264
The model decides what to do next, observes the result, and decides again, until it judges the task done or something stops it.

14
00:01:19.544 --> 00:01:25.368
The question to ask of any system, then, is simple: who chooses the next step, your code or the model?

15
00:01:25.648 --> 00:01:27.995
Everything else in this week follows from that.

16
00:01:28.275 --> 00:01:32.883
Workflows are predictable and cheap to debug because you can read the control flow.

17
00:01:33.163 --> 00:01:43.296
Agents are flexible because they can handle paths you did not anticipate, and they pay for that flexibility in cost, latency, and errors that compound across steps.
